\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Training-free LLM IE.}
\looseness=-1
Training-free LLM IE spans instruction tuning \citep{instructuie-2023}, code-style schema encoding \citep{knowcoder-2024,codeie-2023}, annotation-guided extraction \citep{gollie-2024,adelie-2024}, schema-driven decoding \citep{slot-2025,oneke-2025}, and constrained JSON decoding \citep{json-schema-bench-2025,willard2023efficient}. NER methods include zero-shot decomposition \citep{xie-zeroshot-ner-2023,promptner-2023}, distillation \citep{uniner-2024}, and bidirectional encoders \citep{gliner-2024,gliner2-2025}; RE methods include zero-shot triplet extraction \citep{relationprompt-2022}, constrained generation \citep{josifoski-2022-genie}, and shared embedding spaces \citep{boylan-2025-glirel,wadhwa-re-llm-2023}. These methods perform well with moderate schemas (4--20 types) but none investigates how performance degrades as schema complexity scales.

\paragraph{Schema complexity in IE.}
\looseness=-1
The most directly related work is LLM-PEE \citep{llm-pee-2025}, which partitions large event schemas by type embedding similarity. Our work differs in three ways: (1)~we conduct controlled analysis to understand \emph{why} extraction degrades rather than developing an engineering solution; (2)~our BCS-controlled experiments isolate count from confusability, which LLM-PEE conflates; (3)~our SDGD experiments reveal that merge amplification (not partitioning quality) is the bottleneck in decompose-then-merge pipelines. LLM-PEE's union merge suffers the same precision collapse (0.56$\to$0.21; \S\ref{sec:sdgd}). In short, LLM-PEE assumes decomposition helps; we show why it should (confusability), why it does not (merge amplification), and what predicts the degradation (BCS). Other approaches address schema complexity via hard negative mining \citep{iepile-2024}, schema definition optimization \citep{parse-2025}, multi-agent debate \citep{crossagentie-2025,cmas-ner-2025}, and active prompting \citep{reflect-learn-2026}; all treat it as an engineering problem rather than isolating degradation factors. Concurrently, \citet{chen-2026-multi-instance} show that instance count degrades LLM multi-instance processing more than context length, consistent with our count-dominant finding. \citet{golde-2025-familiarity} quantify training--evaluation type similarity to predict zero-shot NER difficulty; our SCS instead measures within-schema inter-type confusability without assuming access to training data. Label-space reduction approaches \citep{haystack-to-needle-2025,dlisc-2025,spt-2025} implicitly lower batch confusability---our framework explains \emph{why} such reduction works.

\paragraph{Schema complexity in dialogue and IE.}
\looseness=-1
The Schema-Guided Dialogue dataset~\citep{rastogi2020towards} and SGD-X~\citep{lee2022sgd} document degradation with schema linguistic variation; \citet{li2023evaluating} observe count-dependent degradation in ChatGPT IE. The relationship between label similarity and confusion has precedent in visual recognition \citep{dubey-2018-pairwise-confusion}, correlated label noise \citep{collier-2021-correlated-noise}, hierarchical classification \citep{deng-2010-imagenet}, and label confusion matrices \citep{guo-2021-label-confusion,muller2019when,peterson2019human}.

\paragraph{SCS novelty.}
\looseness=-1
SCS inherits the embedding-similarity criterion from LLM-PEE \citep{llm-pee-2025}. The metric is a means; the empirical findings are the contribution, enabled by a controlled experimental methodology that previous work lacked---BCS-controlled batches that isolate confusability from count. The use of embedding distance for type discrimination has parallels in metric learning \citep{snell2017prototypical} and zero-shot classification \citep{yin-2019-zero-shot-bench}. The modest pairwise $r^2$ (1.3--1.7\%) compounds at the ecological batch level to explain ${\sim}$22\% of cross-batch RE variance on FewRel (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

